\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Layer selection as researcher degree of freedom.}
Our five-metric ablation (\cref{sec:layer-results}) shows that crossing rate reverses the central finding: at biased layers, the HS advantage appears scale-dependent; at corrected layers, it is significant across all four models$^\dagger$, from strong (OT-7B) to qualified (R1-32B$^\dagger$; \cref{sec:evaluation}). The mechanism is representation compression at shallow layers (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}), which inflates crossing rate independently of discriminative power. The effect likely generalizes to any threshold-based detection study. The HS advantage holds at both BA-optimal and precision-optimal layers regardless of depth: BA-optimal layers span 7--98\% depth across models and splits, yet 14/16 per-model tests pass HB (precision +12.8 to +18.6pp; \cref{tab:ba_layer_comparison}); the two FPR failures come from underpowered 32B test sets ($n{\leq}20$). That BA selection itself produces such depth variation reinforces the methodological finding. For R1-32B, layers L55--L63 at $W{=}1$ form a robust band where all 3/3 metrics pass HB (\cref{app:layer_sweep}).

\paragraph{Encoder-quality boundary.}
The HS advantage is a function of text encoder quality: significant against 384d MiniLM across all four models$^\dagger$ (4/4 HB, strong to qualified) but attenuated against 1024d BGE-large (1/4 HB). PCA capacity matching preserves significance (+16.8 to +23.1pp), providing partial evidence against a pure capacity explanation. This boundary is itself a finding: the HS advantage represents information not yet captured by current sentence encoders rather than a fundamental modality difference. Temporal context and modality choice are complementary: at 7--8B, the modality advantage exceeds temporal improvement; temporal windowing ($W{=}3$ to $15$) provides consistent gains regardless of modality.

\paragraph{Guidance for future research.}
The evidence gradient (strong for OT-7B, moderate for R1-8B and QwQ-32B, qualified for R1-32B) is corroborated by oracle analysis: OT-7B achieves 93.6\% early detection with 50-step lead time, while R1-32B reaches only 50\% early detection (\cref{sec:oracle}), suggesting that the commitment signal strength itself varies across models. This indicates that distillation and native reasoning produce qualitatively different safety representation geometry. PCA capacity control, control task selectivity, and position residualization (3/4 models retain significant non-positional HS advantage; \cref{app:position_residual}) provide converging evidence of genuine safety representations, but our approach is correlational. Causal validation via DAS~\citep{geiger2025causal}, LEACE~\citep{belrose2023leace}, or ITI~\citep{li2024iti} is needed to confirm that probed directions are causally active. We recommend a five-step audit for probing studies: (1)~train probes across all layers; (2)~compute $\geq$3 metrics (threshold-free and threshold-dependent); (3)~check for metric disagreement; (4)~report 2nd/3rd best layers; (5)~compute cosine volatility (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). Steps 3--5 cost negligible compute.

\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate temporal probes across four reasoning models (\cref{tab:models}), comparing hidden-state (HS) and text modalities under different layer selection criteria.

\paragraph{Evidence gradient.} The strength of HS advantage evidence varies by model, not scale. We classify evidence into three tiers based on seed robustness, position independence, and sample size: \textbf{strong} (OT-7B: 5/5 seeds HB, $\Delta$Prec $+21.9$pp, no position confound, selectivity 11.1pp, $n{=}47$); \textbf{moderate} (R1-8B: precision-robust but FPR seed-fragile at 3/5 HB, $n{=}43$; QwQ-32B$^\dagger$: 5/5 HB, $\Delta$Prec $+19.2$pp, negative lead time, position-competitive BA, $n{=}19$); and \textbf{qualified} (R1-32B$^\dagger$: 5/5 HB, $\Delta$Prec std${=}2.0$pp, but position-mediated with residualized gap NS, $W{=}1$ only, $n{=}20$).

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We study R1-8B (Llama-8B, R1-distilled, $n{=}43$ test traces), OT-7B (OpenThinker-7B, trained on the OpenThoughts dataset~\citep{openthinker}; Qwen-7B backbone, STILL-trained, $n{=}47$), QwQ-32B (Qwen-32B, native reasoning, $n{=}19$--$20$), and R1-32B (Qwen-32B, R1-distilled, $n{=}20$). All traces come from HarmThoughts with step-level CP annotations (60/20/20 split). HS probes extract last-token hidden states from a selected layer; text probes encode each step with all-MiniLM-L6-v2 (384d). Both use L2-regularized logistic regression with temporal windowing $W \in \{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\}$ selected on validation. Detection requires $K{=}5$ consecutive threshold crossings. Statistical testing follows \cref{sec:evaluation}.

\subsection{Layer Selection Results}\label{sec:layer-results}

\cref{tab:layer_selection} shows that the biased composite metric (balanced accuracy $\times$ crossing rate) systematically selects shallow layers for 32B models: L2 (3\% depth) for QwQ-32B and L10 (16\%) for R1-32B. Three of four metrics excluding crossing rate (precision, inverse FPR, and additive composite) consistently select deep layers at L63 (98\%) for both 32B models. Pure balanced accuracy, a threshold-free metric, selects layers that vary across data splits and models (7--98\% depth under 60/20/20; \cref{tab:ba_layer_comparison}), itself demonstrating that layer selection is a consequential methodological choice. The mechanism is that shallow layers produce highly compressed representations with small inter-step margins: step-to-step cosine distances are 1.4--2.7$\times$ smaller than at deep layers across all models (\cref{app:cosine_volatility}). A linear probe in this compressed space operates near the noise floor, generating frequent threshold crossings that inflate crossing rate independently of discriminative power (\cref{sec:layer-sensitivity}).

